    tldr: "A way of converting a physical radiation dose into a single number for comparing radiation risk across different types of radiation and different organs. It is used for radiation-protection purposes, not for prescribing cancer treatment doses.",
    summary: "Equivalent dose (H) adjusts the absorbed dose in an organ or tissue (measured in gray) by a radiation weighting factor (wR) that reflects how biologically damaging a given type of radiation is, compared with photons or beta particles of the same absorbed dose: H_T = sum over R of wR times D_T,R. Current ICRP values are wR = 1 for photons, electrons and beta particles, wR = 2 for protons, and wR = 20 for alpha particles, reflecting alpha particles' much higher biological potency per unit of physical dose. Equivalent dose is expressed in sievert (Sv), the same unit (J/kg) as gray but with a different name to signal that a biological weighting has been applied.\n\nEffective dose (E) goes one step further, multiplying the equivalent dose to each organ by a tissue weighting factor (wT, which sums to 1 across the whole body) that represents that organ's relative contribution to overall cancer and heritable-disease risk from uniform whole-body irradiation, then summing across organs. Effective dose, also in sievert, is the standard quantity used in radiation protection to compare the stochastic (cancer-risk) burden of different exposures, for example a diagnostic PET/CT scan versus natural background radiation.\n\nThese are protection quantities developed by the ICRP for population-level risk management and regulatory dose limits; they are not used to prescribe or evaluate radiopharmaceutical therapy, where the organ-specific absorbed dose in gray (via the MIRD formalism), sometimes adjusted for relative biological effectiveness rather than the fixed regulatory wR, is what guides treatment planning.",
