    id: "linear-energy-transfer",
    kind: "term",
    name: "Linear energy transfer (LET)",
    category: "physics",
    tldr: "A measure of how densely a particle of radiation deposits its energy as it travels through tissue. Alpha particles dump their energy in a short, dense burst; beta particles and gamma rays spread it thinly over a much longer track.",
    summary: "Linear energy transfer (L or LET) is the average energy that a charged ionising particle transfers to the medium per unit length of its track, L = dE/dl, expressed in keV per micrometre (keV/µm). It describes the spatial density of energy deposition (and therefore of ionisation events) along a particle's path, which is what ultimately determines the biological severity of the damage caused for a given absorbed dose.\n\nRepresentative values illustrate the range: cobalt-60 gamma rays and diagnostic x-rays are low-LET (roughly 0.2-2 keV/µm), beta particles from radionuclides such as lutetium-177 are also low-LET (a 1 MeV beta particle deposits roughly 0.2 keV/µm and travels several millimetres), while alpha particles are high-LET, depositing on the order of 80-150 keV/µm over a track of only tens to about a hundred micrometres (a handful of cell diameters). High-LET radiation causes densely clustered ionisations that produce more complex, harder-to-repair DNA double-strand breaks than the sparse ionisation from low-LET beta or gamma radiation, which is the physical basis for targeted alpha therapy's distinct toxicity and efficacy profile compared with beta-emitting radioligand therapy.\n\nLET is the mechanistic quantity underlying both the regulatory radiation weighting factor used in equivalent dose (wR = 20 for alpha versus 1 for beta/gamma) and the empirically measured relative biological effectiveness used to compare therapeutic effect between alpha- and beta-emitting radiopharmaceuticals.",
